6. One architectural change I would make to the larger plan

I would not establish a single global model of what sagacity is.

That would risk replacing individual LLM priors with a stronger, centralized institutional prior.

Instead, I would have Atlas maintain competing computational interpretations, each backed by evidence, with Hades tracking their failure boundaries.

Imagine the system has accumulated three rival explanations of some computational behavior.

It should be able to ask:

Which experiment would most decisively distinguish these interpretations?

And separately:

What kind of mechanism would all three interpretations fail to explain?

That second question is particularly powerful.

We should be able to ask a generator to produce a mechanism that lies outside the demonstrated explanatory reach of existing theories, while still satisfying an independently defined functional contract.

Not simply to generate a new-looking organism.

Not to generate a different neural network.

But to find a computational arrangement that forces the existing scientific ontology to change.

That's a stronger criterion than novelty, and it is much closer to what you've been describing.

7. Where I think we are now

I see three major pieces coming together:

Pan has built the data plane. We no longer need to invent a repository-wide memory system.

Atlas and Hades can provide the evolving scientific representation. The current structured population is small, and the work of constructing it must be scientifically disciplined.

Cosmos can test whether negative knowledge redirects generation. NS-01 should become the first experimental consumer of that structured knowledge rather than another standalone prompt-engineering exercise.

I would postpone LoRA until these three pieces can produce reliable training examples.

But I would also avoid making LoRA contingent on our ability to define sagacity. We could train adapters to improve experimentally measured mechanism diversity and experiment selection long before any universal physics emerges.

The deeper research question

Our starting observation is that LLMs repeatedly gravitate toward familiar structures.

We could frame the program-wide hypothesis this way:

A computational system with persistent experimentally grounded representations of successful mechanisms, characterized failures, and unresolved distinctions can discover new causal structures more efficiently than the same fixed-weight generator operating without that accumulated knowledge.

That claim is falsifiable.

We have the data infrastructure to investigate it.

We have the scientific engines to generate experiments.

And now we know more precisely what's missing: the machinery that converts accumulated failures into experimentally meaningful distinctions and uses them to redirect search.

I would make that one of Prometheus's highest-priority Phase 3 investigations.
